 and new  jersey in march  the florida hospital where she works sat  empty  cleared out to make room for patients  who never came  soon after she was laid off it doesn't  bring me shame but i went through  food donation food drive lines i loaded  the kids up and  grocery money was tight by april an  estimated 1.5 million health care  workers in the u.s had lost their jobs  hospitals canceled money-making elective  surgeries to free up beds for covid19  patients  emergency room visits dropped off as  people stayed home  like any other business for-profit  hospitals looked to cut  costs a lot of hospitals are quiet about  cutting  nurses hours right i mean because who  wants to say during a worldwide pandemic  that you're cutting nurses hours  it's estimated american hospitals have  lost a combined 50 billion dollars per  month  every month since march that was just  the first wave  and as the pandemic ramped up and as  patients started showing up  the costs of treatment spiraled  hospitals went from empty to overwhelmed  forced to buy ppe ventilators and extra  beds  well i think what we're seeing  particularly in the united states is  we're seeing a  system of care that really doesn't act  as a system these are individual  hospitals individual doctors individual  insurance companies  and suppliers all acting in their own  best interests  jobs have been slow to return even as  cases of the virus surpass  4 million it's turned around somewhat  but it hasn't returned  to pre-covered levels  long-term there are fears the financial  crisis gripping the health industry will  lead to a labor shortage  hospitals and clinics could be driven  out of business right now it's ugly  it's it's like nothing i've seen before  today dana james is back on the job  in fact she's busier than ever it kind  of is  it's an eye opener to see how our  country has  dealt with us compared to other  countries and how we're developing  comparing us to the rest of the world  her only worry now tr